1.

I chose in section 1.7 to leave the responsibilty to BrickerGamemManager for deleting bricks in game.
Advantages -
  A. Open/Closed Principle‫:‬ by making BrickergameManager 
responsible for adding removing objects,
 it is easier to extend the program (when I added more objects I just sent them BrickerGameManager, using his methods to control the objects).

B. Composition: Didn't need to repeat myself, all the code needed is in BrickGameManager

C. Abstraction - brick class docent need to know how to remove object, it just calls manager method.

D. Bricker game is responsible for almost everything in program, making it easier to use it.

Disadvantges - 
A. Bricker game manager is a big class, having too much responsibility can be hard in the future to extend the code.

B. Encapsulation is hurt as game manager provides other classes the option to use his methods.

C. If we would like to use brick class in another project, it will be hard adjusting the code to work without our game manager(every DanoGamelab has a game objects Collection, but not every project has a manager like ours)  
      

2.
Life Counter - parent class of all counter. Has a constructor and a remove live, add live and get lives methods. All methods are public (as they called from game manager)

Heart Life Counter - child class of Life Counter, overrides add/remove live methods and constructor, also has a create heart/create hearts private method.

Numeric Life Counter - child class of Life Counter, overrides add/remove live methods and constructor, also has a update color private method.

Life counter uses compostion by having a field for game manager..          

3.
For all strategies I used game manager to create methods in it that each strategy can call when activated.

Extra Live Collision Strategy - called a method in game manger that creates the falling heart. Heart class is responsible for destroying itself if going out of window, and by using should collide with I can check if the heart is colliding with the main paddle.

Extra Paddle Collision Strategy - also called a method in game manager that creats paddle. Ive added a counter to paddle to check if it got 4 hits, also restricted the counter to go up only when colliding with ball/puck. Main paddle counter starts from -1 so it docent get affected from counter. I restricted the ability to only one paddle by adding a field in game manger, numPaddles.

Puck Collision Strategy - also called a method in game manager that creates the pucks. Created a puck class that is a child class of ball to use some of the elements of ball that are similar between the two objects. 

Turbo Mode Collision Strategy - also called a method in game manager that changes ball into turbo mode. used getCollision of ball to keep track when ball should go back to normal.

4.

Ive used a factory to create all strategies, factory gets a counter. If factory chooses to create a Double Behaviors Collision Strategy, so I send the counter to the strategy constructor with game manager and increment it by one. also, the constructor calls factory twice for its two strategies with the counter, if one of strategies is also a Double Behaviors Collision Strategy,  it increments the counter again and then when calling the factory Strategy it dosent include any more Double Behaviors Collision Strategy.

5.

In game manager I added public methods to be called from other classes. 

Ive changed collision strategy to a class because I wanted to use a constructor for all its subclasses (interface docent have one), also, every strategy calls in onCollision the father method (to remove object).

I added in a paddle an onCollisionEnter function to keep track of how many hits the paddle got (for paddle strategy)

Ive added a game constants class in main package to keep track off all constants.
  
  





 